### wait_for_background_work
Wait for background work you started — a bash job, a subagent, or a
    condition — never for a human.

    Wakes on new thread activity, the wake condition passing, background-job
    completion, or the timeout. Subagent completions post a system note to
    this thread, so this wait also wakes when a spawned subagent finishes.
    Short waits resolve inline; a wait that outlasts a few minutes continues
    server-side — your turn ends with wake_reason "parked" and this thread
    resumes automatically when something happens, so never re-issue a wait as
    a timer and never chain timeouts. Once parked, a wait with no condition
    and no job wakes only on thread activity: its timeout expires silently.
    For an obligation at a specific future time schedule a wakeup via the SDK
    (see the wakeups skill). If waiting only for a human — a reply, an
    approval request you just sent, feedback — do not wait: finish the turn;
    their response wakes this thread. If a referenced job already ended, the
    call returns immediately with wake_reason job_finished: read its output
    instead of waiting again.
-- PARAMS: {
 "job_id": {
  "description": "Background bash job to inspect. Defaults to this thread's newest running job.",
  "type": "string"
 },
 "poll_interval_seconds": {
  "default": 5,
  "description": "Seconds between thread and condition checks (floored at 60s once parked)",
  "maximum": 600,
  "minimum": 1,
  "type": "integer"
 },
 "timeout_seconds": {
  "default": 300,
  "description": "Maximum seconds to wait before waking with wake_reason timeout",
  "maximum": 604800,
  "minimum": 1,
  "type": "integer"
 },
 "wake_condition_command": {
  "description": "Optional shell predicate evaluated while waiting. Exit 0 wakes, silent exit 1 keeps waiting, and other results wake with condition_error. It is first evaluated immediately, so it must only pass on new state, not state that already exists. Job paths are available as $BACKGROUND_STDOUT_FILE, $BACKGROUND_STDERR_FILE, and $BACKGROUND_STATUS_FILE.",
  "type": "string"
 }
}
